# Build context for docker/desktop/Dockerfile.
#
# The working tree is ~30 GB, nearly all of it build output. Without this the
# context alone would take longer to send than the image takes to build.
#
# BuildKit reads this file because it is named <dockerfile>.dockerignore.

# Build output
build/
install/
log/
play_log/
tmp/
**/target/

# Data is mounted or downloaded, never copied: the rosbag alone is ~934 MB and
# would land in a layer that invalidates on every source change.
data/
rosbags/

# Exported images. `export-images.sh` writes its tarballs next to itself by
# default, and they are the image -- 19 GB of it. Without this line the context
# carries the previous build INTO the next one: measured, a desktop image that
# should be 26.7 GB came out at 46.1 GB, all of it in `COPY . /opt/AutoSDV`,
# because two handout tarballs were sitting in docker/desktop/ at the time.
# `**/` is not decoration: a .dockerignore pattern anchors at the context ROOT,
# so a bare `*.tar.gz` matches nothing in docker/desktop/, which is exactly
# where export-images.sh writes. Measured the wrong way round first -- the
# rebuild came out 46.1 GB again with the bare pattern in place.
**/*.tar.gz
**/*.tar.gz.sha256
**/*.tar

# Version control
.git
**/.git
.github/

# Editor and local state
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
record.json
resume.org

# HOST setup state. This one matters: setup.sh imports .markers/ and concludes
# the step already ran, so a build that copies this host's markers SKIPS
# installing ROS 2 and Autoware and produces a broken image without an error.
# The Dockerfile also deletes these, because a mistake here is silent.
setup/.markers/
setup/.state.json
**/.markers/

# direnv state belongs to the host checkout
.envrc
.direnv/

# The site build of the book, if anyone has one here
site/
